#Part 1: In-Class

data(BEPS)

##1.1 Ordered logit

#a)
table(BEPS$economic.cond.national)
BEPS$econ_ord = factor(BEPS$economic.cond.national, ordered = TRUE)

#OLS treats the numerical values 1–5 as equally spaced, suggesting that the difference between 
#got much worse and got a little worse is the same as the difference between stayed the same and got a little better.
#The majority of respondents answered 2, 3, or 4, with 3 being the most selected category. The extremes (1 and 5) are by far the least selected categories. 
#For Likert-type survey items, when psychological distances between neighboring categories need not be equal, this is most likely incorrect.

#b)
m_ologit = polr(econ_ord ~ age + gender + Europe + political.knowledge,
                data = BEPS, Hess = TRUE)
summary(m_ologit)

#c)
avg_slopes(m_ologit)

#d)
predictions(m_ologit, newdata = datagrid(gender = c("female", "male")))

##1.2 Multinomial logit

#a)
BEPS$vote = relevel(BEPS$vote, ref = "Conservative")
m_mlogit = multinom(vote ~ economic.cond.national + Blair + Hague +
                      Kennedy + Europe, data = BEPS, trace = FALSE)
summary(m_mlogit)

#b)
avg_slopes(m_mlogit)

predictions(m_mlogit, by = "economic.cond.national")

#If people have a negative assessment of the economy (1 or 2), they are 61.8% likely to vote for labor.

#c)
#Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives (IIA) is assumed by the multinomial logit:
#The existence or characteristics of the third alternative (Liberal Democrats) have no bearing on the odds ratio between any two options (Labour, Conservative).
#IIA fails in the red bus/blue bus comparison because the two options are almost perfect replacements, and eliminating one just transfers the probability to the other instead of dispersing it proportionately. 
#IIA is a modest concern for British party choice:
#Since the Liberal Democrats and Labor are both center-left parties with similar ideologies, some people could view them as partial replacements in a way that IIA cannot accept. 
#However, the three-party menu is not as degenerate as two buses of different colors since the Conservatives hold a distinctly right-wing ideological perspective.
#In general, IIA is a more reasonable concern for the Labor/Liberal Democrat divide, although it is conceivable for Conservatives compared to the others.

#1.3 Poisson regression

data(bioChemists)

#a)
summary(bioChemists$art)
var(bioChemists$art)

pdf("art_histogram.pdf", width = 6, height = 4)
hist(bioChemists$art, breaks = 20, main = "Distribution of articles",
     xlab = "Number of articles", col = "purple")
dev.off()


ggplot(bioChemists, aes(x = art)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 1, fill = "#9924a1", color = "yellow") +
  theme_minimal() +
  labs(title = "Publications in last 3 years of PhD",
       x = "Number of articles", y = "Count")

#The variance is around 3.71, while the mean is 1.69.

#b)
m_pois = glm(art ~ fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment,
             data = bioChemists, family = poisson)
summary(m_pois)

#c)
dispersiontest(m_pois)

#The dispersion is around 1.82, and the p-value is p<0.001.
#Because the model underestimates uncertainty, inflates test statistics, and generates p-values that are deceptively tiny, the Poisson standard errors are too little.

